 "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0,
 "summary": "Analysis of pyasn1 0.6.1 (a pure-Python ASN.1 library with BER/CER/DER codecs). I ran a small number of CodeQL queries, and several of them failed to compile or returned too much output. The results are heuristic and I did not confirm them with a proof of concept.\n\nFindings:\n1. **Possible memory-exhaustion DoS in OID and RELATIVE-OID decoding (CWE-400, CWE-770, medium).**\n   - Location: `pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py`, in the OID and RELATIVE-OID `valueDecoder`s.\n   - The while loops at about lines 421-438 (OBJECT IDENTIFIER) and 479-496 (RELATIVE-OID) accumulate arithmetic (`AugAssign`) over continuation octets.\n   - I saw no bound on the number of continuation octets or on the size of the resulting integer, so a crafted payload could consume a lot of memory or CPU.\n   - This matches the publicly known weakness fixed in pyasn1 0.6.2.\n2. **Unbounded accumulation in other BER decoder loops (CWE-400, low).**\n   - A query for accumulation in loops flagged about 20 spots in the same file. They include the indefinite-length decoders (around lines 358, 888, 1114 and 1381) and the stream `__call__` and `__iter__` paths (around lines 1608-1620 and 1974).\n   - Whether input length or nesting depth is capped in these paths was not confirmed.\n3. **Dynamic `getattr` in `pyasn1/type/base.py` (line 222) (low, informational).**\n   - I did not see attacker-controlled input reach it.\n\nOther results:\n- No code execution, command execution, deserialization or SQL sinks were found.\n- The package does no network or file handling beyond its stream readers.\n\nRecommendation: upgrade to a release that bounds OID and RELATIVE-OID continuation octets (0.6.2 or later). Applications should also cap the size of untrusted ASN.1 input before decoding it.",
